  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In the following reaction</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0079_922e1b832230.png\" style=\"width: 380px; height: 130px;\" />​​​​​​​</p><p>The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math> yield for reaction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">I</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></math> and that of reaction II is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></math>. The overall yield of the complete reaction is<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "30",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<blockquote><p><img src=\"assets\\st0079_7cdb59200eff.png\" style=\"width: 281px; height: 100px;\" /></p><p>Let initial moles of reactant taken <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math><br />Total moles obtained for benzene sulphonic acid (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math> yield <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>%</mo></math>)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math></p><p><img src=\"assets\\st0079_0411d4df1065.png\" style=\"width: 262px; height: 120px;\" /></p><p>Moles of benzene sulphonic acid before reaction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>II</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math></p><p>Moles obtained for phenol (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math> yield = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>50</mn><mo>%</mo></math>)&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></math></p><p>So over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>%</mo></math>&nbsp;yield of complete reaction&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mfrac><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>30</mn></math></p></blockquote>"
  }
}